### Using the information to solve

In order to find $c'(t_0)$ we need to find $c(t_0)$. Using the Pythagorean theorem and the fact that $a(t_0)=9$ and $b=12$, we can find that $c(t_0)=15$.

Now let's plug $\blueD{a(t_0)}=\blueD{9}$, $\maroonD{a'(t_0)}=\maroonD{4}$, and $\goldD{c(t_0)}=\goldD{15}$ into the expression for $c'(t_0)$:

$\begin{align}
c'(t_0)&=\dfrac{\blueD{a(t_0)}\maroonD{a'(t_0)}}{\goldD{c(t_0)}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{(\blueD{9})(\maroonD{4})}{(\goldD{15})}
\\\\
&=2.4
\end{align}$
